The "history" concept in your chatbot

The core idea is conversation memory. LLM APIs are stateless — each call to client.chat.completions.create() has zero memory of previous calls. If you don't send the prior messages back, the model has no idea what "that" or "it" refers to in a follow-up question.

Your this.history array solves this by keeping a running transcript:

js
this.history = [{role: "system", content: systemPrompt}]

Three roles, each with a distinct job:

system — sent once, sets the persona/rules ("you are a friendly travel agent"). The model treats this as its instructions, not something to respond to.
user — what the human says.
assistant — what the model replied. Pushing this back into history is what lets the model "remember" its own earlier answers.

So each chat() call does three things:

Appends the new user message to history.
Sends the entire history so far (not just the latest message) to the API.
Appends the model's reply to history, so it's included next time.

That's why in your main(), the second call ("How many days...") correctly resolves to Switzerland — the model can see the full prior exchange, not just the isolated question.

One thing worth knowing: history grows unbounded. Long conversations eventually hit the model's context window limit and cost more tokens per call. Production chatbots usually truncate old messages, summarize them, or cap history length — worth mentioning if you extend this.

In one sentence: The probability distribution is just the model's ranked list of "here's how likely each possible next word is," and it picks from that list to keep writing.




