Large Language Models (LLMs)

What they are:
An LLM is a neural network — almost always built on the Transformer architecture — trained on massive amounts of text to predict the next word (technically, the next "token") in a sequence. That simple objective, when scaled up with billions of parameters and trillions of words of training data, produces a model that can write, reason, translate, summarize, code, and hold conversations.

How they work (core ideas):

Tokenization — text is broken into small chunks (tokens), not always whole words.
Embeddings — tokens are converted into vectors (lists of numbers) that capture meaning.
Self-attention — the key Transformer innovation. It lets the model weigh how much every word in a sentence should "pay attention to" every other word, capturing context and relationships regardless of distance.
Layers — dozens of attention + feed-forward layers stacked on top of each other refine the model's understanding.
Training phases:
Pre-training — predicting the next token on huge internet-scale text corpora.
Fine-tuning / instruction tuning — teaching the model to follow instructions.
RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) — aligning outputs with human preferences (helpful, harmless, honest).

Key characteristics:

LLMs are stateless — they don't remember previous conversations unless that history is fed back in as context.
They have a context window — a limit on how much text they can "see" at once.
They can hallucinate — generate plausible-sounding but incorrect information, since they predict likely text, not verified facts.
Examples: GPT-4/5, Claude, Gemini, LLaMA, Mistral.

Limitations that matter in practice:

No built-in access to real-time data or private company data.
No persistent memory across sessions (unless engineered separately).
Can't natively take actions in the world (send emails, query databases) — they just generate text.

This is exactly the gap that frameworks like LangChain were built to fill.

LangChain

What it is:
LangChain is an open-source framework for building applications powered by LLMs. Instead of just calling an LLM API and getting text back, LangChain gives you the plumbing to connect an LLM to external data, tools, memory, and multi-step logic — turning a "text predictor" into a functional application component.

Core building blocks:

Models — a unified interface to call different LLMs (OpenAI, Anthropic, open-source models, etc.) interchangeably.
Prompts / Prompt Templates — reusable, parameterized prompt structures so you're not hand-writing prompts every time.
Chains — the namesake concept: sequences of calls (LLM → parse output → feed into next step → etc.) linked together to accomplish multi-step tasks.
Memory — mechanisms to persist conversation history or state across turns, since LLMs themselves are stateless. Ranges from simple buffer memory to summarized memory for long conversations.
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) — arguably LangChain's most popular use case:
Your documents are split into chunks.
Each chunk is converted into an embedding and stored in a vector database (e.g., Pinecone, Chroma, FAISS, Weaviate).
When a user asks a question, LangChain retrieves the most relevant chunks and stuffs them into the prompt as context.
The LLM then answers using your actual data, not just what it memorized during training — this reduces hallucination and lets you query private/internal knowledge.
Agents — instead of a fixed chain, an agent lets the LLM decide which tool to use and in what order, based on the task. Tools can include web search, calculators, APIs, code execution, databases, etc. The LLM acts as a reasoning engine that plans and calls tools dynamically (often using patterns like ReAct — Reason + Act).
Tools — wrappers around external functionality (search engines, calculators, SQL databases, custom APIs) that agents can invoke.
LangGraph — a newer companion library for building more controllable, stateful, graph-based agent workflows (loops, branching, human-in-the-loop checkpoints) — used when simple linear chains aren't enough.

Why it matters:
LangChain essentially turns an LLM from a "smart autocomplete" into the reasoning core of a real application — one that can search the web, query your company's database, remember a conversation, or orchestrate multi-step workflows like "research a topic, summarize it, and email the summary."

Typical use cases:

Chatbots with access to your own documents (RAG)
AI agents that can browse, call APIs, and take actions
Document summarization and analysis pipelines
Customer support bots grounded in a knowledge base
Multi-step research/report-generation assistants
